MV(1)                          P32W Unix                           MV(1)

NAME
       mv - move or rename files

SYNOPSIS
       mv old new
       mv file ... directory

DESCRIPTION
       mv gives the file old the name new. If new already exists it
       is replaced. If the last argument is a directory, each file
       keeps its last path component and moves into that
       directory: mv a/x d is mv a/x d/x, and mv x y z d moves all
       three.

       Within one filesystem mv renames (see rename(2)), so the
       file's inode, owner, mode and times are unchanged. Moving a
       file to itself, by any path (mv x ./x), does nothing.

       To another filesystem (such as one mounted on /mnt) a plain
       file is copied, with its mode and modification time (and,
       for root, its owner), and the original then removed. If the
       copy fails it is removed and the original kept.

       Only plain files (and devices, within a filesystem) can be
       moved; mv refuses directories ("mv: d: cannot move a
       directory").

OPTIONS
       --     ends the options, for a name that starts with -.

EXAMPLES
       mv draft.txt letter.txt       rename a file
       mv letter.txt /usr/ken        move it into ken's home
       mv *.o /mnt/old               several, to another filesystem

EXIT STATUS
       0 if every file was moved; 1 if any could not be (mv says why
       and goes on with the next), or if there are more than two
       arguments and the last is not a directory; 2 for a usage
       error (fewer than two arguments, an option).

SEE ALSO
       cp(1), ln(1), rm(1), rename(2), link(2), unlink(2)

BUGS
       There are no -f or -i options and no prompting.

       Directories cannot be renamed. A device cannot be moved to
       another filesystem.
